by nicolas-grekas at 2026-07-28T08:25:37Z

Review notes, for the record. Three things were changed on top of the submitted patch.

1. Constructor arguments were not covered. `AbstractNormalizer::instantiateObject()` reads the serialized key and removes it from the working data before the attribute loop runs, so the guard never saw that key and the raw key still overwrote the value the constructor had received. The data is now snapshotted before instantiation and the guard tests against that snapshot.

2. The guard ran before the existing extra-attributes detection, so with `allow_extra_attributes` set to `false` a payload carrying both keys stopped raising `ExtraAttributesException` and was accepted silently. The two blocks are now ordered the other way around, which keeps strict mode exactly as it is on 6.4.

3. The two `@return mixed` docblocks that the patch removed are restored. They only exist on 6.4, where these methods have no native return type, so they carry the only type information available.

Tests now cover both key orders, the constructor case, and strict mode still raising. Verified in three states: on plain 6.4 the two precedence tests fail and the strict-mode test passes, on the submitted patch the constructor test and the strict-mode test fail, and all pass with the current diff. The Serializer, HttpKernel, Messenger, PropertyInfo, Mime and FrameworkBundle suites are green.

The merge into 7.4 is conflict free and the Serializer suite passes there too, so no resolution is needed on merge up. The tests avoid a data provider on purpose: 6.4 runs PHPUnit 9 and 7.4 runs PHPUnit 13, where docblock providers no longer work, so a conflict-free merge would have carried up a test that does not run.

